# 19. OLTP

The shop's systems so far are its **OLTP** side: online transaction
processing, the side where things happen. An order is placed and a row is
committed; a payment fails and a message is published; the warehouse reads
the next shipment and acknowledges it. Each fact is one row or one message,
written once as it occurs, and what the system holds is the current state of
the business: these orders, these customers, this stock. The PostgreSQL
table, the Kafka topic and the JetStream subject of chapters
[[16-tables-and-topics|16]] and [[17-subjects-and-the-lakehouse|17]]
are all of this kind, and so is the first lakehouse table the raw orders land
in.

The pipelines of this side share a shape. They read one value at a time,
`tap`, and write one value at a time, `sink`, as each arrives; they decide
each value on its own, `validate`, `filter`, `map`, and keep nothing
between two values; and they run for as long as values arrive. The
guarantees they rest on are the ones of [[16-tables-and-topics|chapter 16]]
and [[17-subjects-and-the-lakehouse|chapter 17]]: a row committed as it
is written, a message stored before the write ends, a message delivered at
least once and so written so that twice does no harm.

This chapter is the shop's whole transactional day as one file, and what
follows from its being a file. The other side, the history those
transactions leave and the questions a report asks of it, is
[[20-olap|chapter 20]].

